7. Mr. Hexry Ward Poole's Organ.

¶ p. 323d, note f, and App. XVIII. p. 423a, for references to Mr. Poole's ¶ His papers are in Silliman's American Journal of Arts and Sciences, ¶ vol. ix. pp. 68-8.3, 199-216; 1867, vol. xliv. pp. 1-22 (which contains the ¶ of his keyboard, here reproduced as figs. 68, 69, 70, p. 475, by the photo¶ processes of the Typo-etching Company); 1868, vol. xlv. p. 289. The ¶ papers contain Mr. Poole's theory and an account of his Enharmonic Organ, ¶ by himself and Mr. Joseph Abbey, of Newburyport, Massachusetts, ¶ an ordinary fingerboard and a pedal to change the pipes that it affected, and ¶ from the major key of D\f with 5 flats to that of B with 5 sharps. The 12 ¶ brought into action by each pedal produced the 7 notes of the major scale, ¶ leading note of the relative minor scale, the perfect Seventh, and three others ¶ to adjoining scales, of which only one (the grave Second) is specified, ¶ This arrangement, which was actually used in Boston, was abandoned, because it ¶ found advisable to have all the notes under command of the hand with¶ pedal action, and to use pedals for the bass only. But the new keyboard in ¶ complete form does not appear from Mr. Poole's papers to have advanced ¶ the stage of a cardboard model, although more recent simplifications, ¶ 24 and 48 tones to the Octave, have been practically worked out. To these ¶ will be made at the conclusion of this notice.

¶ his theory of this keyboard, to which all subsequent remarks refer, Mr. Poole ¶ 5 series of Fifths, namely those in cols. 5, 6, 7 of the Duodenarium, ¶ 463, and two others interposed which may be numbered as '5 and "6, because they ¶ notes which are a septimal comma (63 : 64, or 27 cents) flatter than the ¶ notes in cols. 5 and 6. These are given in the following duodenary ¶ of his notes. But instead of my superior and inferior numbers he ¶ varieties of type, as shewn in the letterpress below fig. 70, which was photo¶ from the original at the same time as the fig. itself.

